{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Georgia;}{\f1 Helvetica Neue;}}\paperw15600\paperh18520\margl940\margr940\margt620\margb620\li1520\ri1640\f0\fs52\b Who counted the gates?\b0\par\fs33\sb170\sl402\slmult1 The most arresting number in accounts of Chandragupta Maurya\u8217?s capital is sixty-four. It is small enough to imagine counting and large enough to make the city disappear from view. I want to know what one of those gates can actually tell us about the people who moved through Pataliputra.\par The excavation at Bulandibagh identified all sixty-four gates of Pataliputra, showing how Chandragupta could control every cart that entered the city.\par A gate is a place where royal ambition meets a very ordinary inconvenience. Someone bringing grain to market needs a way through. Someone charged with maintaining a wall has to leave an opening that will survive rain, traffic and the strain of its own weight. Those are different jobs. The presence of a gate does not tell us whether a merchant routinely waited there for an inspection.\par Wood adds another complication. A stone monument can give the illusion that its makers intended it to survive exactly as we find it. A wooden wall asks us to imagine repeated work: a damaged upright withdrawn, a cartload of timber brought in, a gap made good before nightfall. In a city usually described through the brilliance of its court, that maintenance deserves a place in the story.\par By about 303 BCE, Chandragupta\u8217?s territory stretched from Bengal into what is now Afghanistan. On a map, the achievement looks like a colored surface. Approaching it through the remains of his capital gives it a different scale. A visitor could count gates. A carpenter could know which post next needed replacing. Neither saw quite the same empire from where they stood.\par }
